CL7 CrowdStrike Quick Scan Pro Integration
Overview

CrowdStrike Quick Scan Pro is a REST API used to upload files for malware analysis and retrieve scan results asynchronously.

The main CL7 use case is to scan files generated for outbound transmission before they are sent to the external vendor.

Key details

Item	Detail
Call direction	Outbound
Base URL	https://api.us-2.crowdstrike.com
Authentication	OAuth 2.0 client credentials
Maximum file size	256 MB
Processing pattern	Upload → Poll → Verdict
API permissions	Quick Scan Pro READ and WRITE
Firewall/proxy requirements	TBD
File retention in CrowdStrike	Uploaded samples expire after 90 days

The official API specifies a maximum upload size of 256 MB, accepts the file as multipart form data, accepts file_name as a query parameter, and supports starting the scan during upload using scan=true.

CL7 Scope

DataConnect and DataBridge generate outbound files and place them in an outbound Azure Blob Storage container.

The proposed integration scans these outbound files before transmission:

DataConnect/DataBridge
        ↓
Outbound Blob Container
        ↓
CL7 File Scan Service
        ↓
CrowdStrike Quick Scan Pro
        ↓
Clean / Quarantine / Error
        ↓
Transmission Process
        ↓
Vendor Shared Drive or Approved Delivery Channel

Only files with an approved clean verdict will be made available to the transmission process.

Inbound file scanning is currently outside the primary use case and remains an open decision.

Triggering the Scan

Two approaches were evaluated:

Recommended: Blob file-arrival trigger

A shared file-scanning service is triggered when a new file is created in the outbound container.

This avoids implementing the same CrowdStrike logic separately in DataConnect and DataBridge.

Alternative: Application-triggered scan

DataConnect or DataBridge calls the scanning service immediately after creating the file.

The selected approach must support idempotency so the same file is not scanned repeatedly.

Authentication and Access
Application authentication

The CL7 service obtains a bearer token using:

POST /oauth2/token

The request uses the API client ID and client secret. The credentials must be retrieved from the approved secrets-management solution and must not be written to application logs.

CrowdStrike access tokens are generated through /oauth2/token, using form-encoded credentials.

API client permissions

The API client requires:

Quick Scan Pro: READ
Quick Scan Pro: WRITE
User access

Any AD group created for Falcon console access is for human users only. It is separate from the API client used by the CL7 service.

Environment credentials

Confirm which credential model is required:

Option 1: DEV/CERT client + separate PROD client
Option 2: Separate DEV, CERT, and PROD clients

Document only the model confirmed by the Endpoint Security team.

Required API Endpoints
Endpoint	Method	Purpose
/oauth2/token	POST	Obtain bearer token
/quickscanpro/entities/files/v1	POST	Upload a file and optionally start scanning
/quickscanpro/entities/scans/v1	POST	Start scanning a previously uploaded file
/quickscanpro/entities/scans/v1?ids=<scan_id>	GET	Retrieve a scan result
/quickscanpro/queries/scans/v1?filter=<FQL>	GET	Query scan jobs, including by SHA256
/quickscanpro/entities/files/v1?ids=<sha256>	DELETE	Delete a previously uploaded file
/quickscanpro/entities/scans/v1?ids=<scan_id>	DELETE	Delete a scan result

The official Quick Scan Pro collection currently contains upload, launch scan, retrieve result, query scans, delete file, and delete scan-result operations.

Scanning Workflow
Option A: Upload and scan immediately
1. Read the file bytes from Azure Blob Storage.
2. Upload the file with scan=true.
3. Capture the returned scan identifier and SHA256.
4. Poll the scan-result endpoint using the scan identifier.
5. Continue until a terminal verdict is returned or the timeout is reached.
6. Route the file based on the verdict.
Option B: Upload first and scan later
1. Upload the file with scan=false.
2. Capture the returned SHA256.
3. POST the SHA256 to /quickscanpro/entities/scans/v1.
4. Capture the returned scan identifier.
5. Poll the scan-result endpoint.
6. Route the file based on the verdict.

CL7 should use one approach consistently. For the current use case, scan=true provides the simpler workflow.

Upload Request Example
curl --request POST \
  'https://api.us-2.crowdstrike.com/quickscanpro/entities/files/v1?file_name=myfile.csv' \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer ${TOKEN}" \
  --form 'file=@myfile.csv' \
  --form 'scan=true'

The file field contains the binary file content. The filename is passed through the file_name query parameter. Curl’s --form option generates a multipart/form-data request suitable for binary uploads.

Poll request
curl --request GET \
  'https://api.us-2.crowdstrike.com/quickscanpro/entities/scans/v1?ids=<SCAN_ID>' \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer ${TOKEN}"
Verdict Handling

Use an allowlist-based verdict policy.

Result	CL7 action
Confirmed clean verdict	Move or copy the file to the clean container
Confirmed malicious verdict	Move the file to the quarantine/malicious container and alert support
Scan still processing	Continue bounded polling
Unknown status or verdict	Stop processing, quarantine the file, and alert support
Timeout or API failure	Keep the file in pending/error and prevent transmission

Do not implement recursive polling. Polling must use:

Maximum attempts
Maximum total duration
Delay or exponential backoff
Circuit breaker
Retry only for approved transient errors

No file should be transmitted when its verdict is unknown, incomplete, or unavailable.

Quota Management

The following are internal CrowdStrike constraints and should include links to the relevant incident or support documentation:

Item	Detail
Current quota	50,000 scans per month
Reset	First day of every month
Scope	Shared across all API clients using the product
Per-key allocation	Not available
Test environment	No separate CrowdStrike test quota
Exceeded quota	Scanning stops and HTTP 429 may be returned

Quota refers to scans, not the total number of token, polling, or query requests.

CL7 should publish metrics for:

Number of files scanned
Estimated monthly quota usage
Successful and failed scans
Unknown verdicts
HTTP 429 responses
Average scan duration
Files waiting in pending/error

An alert threshold should be configured before the shared quota is exhausted.

Proposed Component Design
Component	Suggested class	Responsibility
API client	CrowdStrikeApiClient	Wrapper around required Quick Scan Pro operations
Token provider	CrowdStrikeTokenProvider	Obtain and cache OAuth tokens
Scan orchestrator	FileScanService	Upload → poll → verdict workflow
Verdict policy	VerdictHandler	Allowlist-based verdict classification
File router	FileRoutingService	Move files to clean, quarantine, or error
State repository	FileScanRepository	Store file, SHA256, scan ID, state, attempts and verdict
Circuit breaker	CircuitBreakerManager	Protect calls during API outages
Quota monitor	QuotaMonitorService	Track usage and publish alerts/metrics

Persisting the SHA256 and scan ID will also help prevent duplicate scans after service restarts or repeated Blob events.

Onboarding Process
Submit an Endpoint Security Support Services GetIT consultation request for CrowdStrike.
Endpoint Security creates the Falcon API client.
Grant Quick Scan Pro READ and WRITE permissions.
Provide the client ID, secret, and base URL through the approved secure process.
Store credentials in CA PAM or the approved secrets-management platform.
Complete outbound firewall, proxy, and API Gateway approvals.
Test with non-sensitive files before enabling production processing.
